-- Passage wg11-argument-writing-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('69d75746-53fe-5b5e-9e31-a8ecdacb15e9', 'wg11-argument-writing-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g11-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Harlow Street Depot$q$, $q$(1) The Harlow Street Depot, a brick rail station built in 1911, has stood empty since passenger service to Kessington ended in 1974. (2) Old buildings are interesting. (3) The depot is the last surviving structure from the period when Kessington grew from a farming village into a regional market town, and its waiting room still contains the original ticket windows and tile floor. (4) A 2023 engineering review commissioned by the county found that the foundation and roof framing are sound. (5) Restoring the depot as a local history museum and rentable community hall would preserve that record while giving the building a practical use.

(6) A developer has proposed demolishing the depot to build forty apartments. (7) Kessington does need more housing, and this need should not be dismissed. (8) The developer's own site plan shows that the apartments could be built on the vacant lot directly behind the depot, leaving the station standing. (9) Some residents suggest saving only the front wall of the depot and building new space behind it. (10) This approach keeps the building's appearance but loses the interior, which is the part that tells the town's story.

(11) My grandmother remembers taking the train from the depot to visit her sister. (12) In the end, Kessington can have both new housing and its history if the council requires the apartments to use the rear lot and funds the depot's restoration.$q$, 'draft')
