   $q$Sentence (10) asserts that review would support referees but offers no evidence. Which revision best develops it while keeping an objective tone?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Review could support referees: in a survey by the league's officials' association, most referees said they would welcome a way to correct errors before a result became final.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Video review would make referees perfect and end every dispute about calls.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overstated-claim"},{"text":"Anyone who thinks review hurts referees has obviously never refereed a game.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"dismissive-tone"},{"text":"Referees deserve respect, and respect is very important in every sport.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"circular-reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Review could support referees: in a survey by the league's officials' association, most referees said they would welcome a way to correct errors before a result became final.$q$, $q$The correct revision adds evidence from referees themselves, which directly answers the concern in sentence (9). The claim that review would make referees perfect overstates what the evidence can support.$q$, 'draft', '175c0575-5051-5d8e-99de-5188c2c476d4')
